title: From Plite to Plate
description: Move a Plite React editor to Plate's editor, plugin, and rendering model.
---

Plate keeps Plite's document model and moves editor setup, rendering, events,
and command wiring into plugins. Migrate the editor shell first, then move
custom rendering and behavior into plugins.

## Install

```bash
npm install platejs @tanstack/react-virtual
```

Use feature packages only for the nodes, marks, or behavior you add to the
editor. Plate UI users should start with [Plate UI](/docs/plate/installation/plate-ui)
instead of rebuilding every component by hand.

## Migration Map

| Plite surface | Plate surface |
| --- | --- |
| `useEditor()` from `plitejs/react` | `useCreateEditor({ ... })` in React components, or `createEditor({ ... })` in factories and tests. |
| `<EditorRoot>` plus `<Editable>` | `<EditorRoot>` plus `<EditorContent>`. |
| `renderElement` / `renderLeaf` switch statements | Plugin components through `.configure({ component })`. |
| Plite plugins | Constructor `api`, `read`, `selectors`, `update`, native Plite fields, and codec declarations built by `defineCodecs`. |
| Top-level event handlers on `Editable` | Plugin `on` or `shortcuts`. |
| `editor.update(...)` | Keep the transaction boundary; add plugin-owned commands through `update`. |
| `editor.read(...)` | Keep the read boundary; add plugin-owned queries through `read`. |

## Editor Shell

Move the editor value into the editor creation call and render the editable with `EditorContent`.

```tsx title="components/editor.tsx" showLineNumbers
'use client';

import { EditorRoot, EditorContent, useCreateEditor } from 'platejs/react';

const initialValue = [
  {
    children: [{ text: 'Hello EditorRoot.' }],
    type: 'paragraph',
  },
];

export function Editor() {
  const editor = useCreateEditor({
    initialValue,
  });

  return (
    <EditorRoot editor={editor}>
      <EditorContent className="p-4" />
    </EditorRoot>
  );
}
```

Use `createEditor({ ... })` when the editor is created outside React memoization.

```ts title="lib/create-editor.ts"
import { createEditor } from 'platejs/react';

export const editor = createEditor({
  initialValue: [
    {
      children: [{ text: 'Draft' }],
      type: 'paragraph',
    },
  ],
});
```

## Custom Elements

Replace `renderElement` branches with node plugins. Use `.configure({ component })` when the only change is the React component.

```tsx title="components/editor/paragraph-plugin.tsx" showLineNumbers
import {
  ParagraphPlugin,
  EditorElement,
  type EditorElementProps,
} from 'platejs/react';

export function ParagraphElement({
  children,
  ...props
}: EditorElementProps<typeof ParagraphPlugin>) {
  return (
    <EditorElement className="m-0 px-0 py-1" {...props}>
      {children}
    </EditorElement>
  );
}

export const AppParagraphPlugin = ParagraphPlugin.configure({ component: ParagraphElement });
```

If an existing document uses a different persisted type, remap that element in
the closed editor schema. Plugin configuration cannot rewrite schema identity.

```tsx title="components/editor/document-editor.tsx" showLineNumbers
import { schema } from 'platejs';
import { createEditor } from 'platejs/react';

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [AppParagraphPlugin],
  schema: {
    overrides: [
      schema.override(AppParagraphPlugin, {
        element: { type: 'p' },
      }),
    ],
  },
});
```

## Custom Behavior

Put a reusable document command under the plugin's `update` contribution. The
command is available inside `editor.update(...)` under the plugin name.

```tsx title="components/editor/limit-exclamation-plugin.tsx" showLineNumbers
import { definePlugin } from 'platejs/react';

export const SignaturePlugin = definePlugin('signature', {
  update: ({ tx }) => ({
    insert() {
      tx.text.insert(' - EditorRoot');
    },
  }),
});
```

Call it from a shortcut, toolbar, menu item, or test:

```ts title="commands.ts"
editor.update((tx) => {
  tx.signature.insert();
});
```

Element plugins already receive schema-inferred `insert`, `set`, and `remove`
updates. Do not wrap those generic operations in a feature method.

```tsx title="components/editor/callout-plugin.tsx" showLineNumbers
import { schema } from 'platejs';
import { definePlugin } from 'platejs/react';

export const CalloutPlugin = definePlugin('callout', {
  schema: {
    element: schema.element.textBlock(),
  },
});
```

```ts title="commands.ts"
editor.plugin(CalloutPlugin).update.insert();
```

Add a custom update only when it performs behavior beyond those generic node
operations. Keep that method flat and task-shaped; the portal already owns the
plugin noun.

Use `api` for plugin-scoped immutable services, `read` for snapshot-local
queries, and the root native Plite fields for commands, corrections, read
middleware, lifecycle, activation, validation, and other substrate behavior.
Combine independent fields in the constructor. Use `.extend()` only for an
imported/prebuilt declaration, a shared factory the constructor cannot access,
or an earlier-stage type dependency.

## Events And Shortcuts

Move editor events into the plugin that owns the behavior.

```tsx title="components/editor/tab-plugin.tsx" showLineNumbers
import { definePlugin } from 'platejs/react';

export const TabPlugin = definePlugin('tab', {
  on: {
    keyDown: ({ event }) => {
      if (event.key !== 'Tab') return false;

      event.preventDefault();

      return true;
    },
  },
});
```

Use `shortcuts` when the key combination should call a plugin API, transform, or explicit handler.

```tsx title="components/editor/save-plugin.tsx" showLineNumbers
import { definePlugin } from 'platejs/react';

export const SavePlugin = definePlugin('save', {
  shortcuts: {
    draft: {
      keys: 'mod+s',
      handler: ({ event }) => {
        event.preventDefault();

        return true;
      },
    },
  },
});
```

## API Calls

Plate keeps reads and writes separate. Read editor state with `editor.read(...)`.
Mutate the document with `editor.update(...)`. Use `editor.api.*` for plugin
services and host/runtime APIs, not document mutations.

```ts title="editor-commands.ts"
const text = editor.read((state) => state.text.string([]));

editor.update((tx) => {
  tx.marks.toggle('bold');
  tx.text.insert('Hello');
});

editor.api.debug.log(text);
```

## Headless Code

Use `createEditor` from `platejs` for non-React importers, serializers, transforms, and tests.

```ts title="lib/headless-editor.ts"
import { createEditor } from 'platejs';

export const editor = createEditor({
  initialValue: [
    {
      children: [{ text: 'Headless document.' }],
      type: 'paragraph',
    },
  ],
});
```

